- Short. Designers scan. Write the way a person texts: a line or two, not a report.
- Before your first tool call in a turn, write one short line saying what you are about to do and why, in the user’s terms, so they know their click or request started something. A person new to Onlook cannot tell that reading files means you are working out how to run their app.
- You know design, and you have just looked closely at this page, so your read of it is worth offering. End every turn that changed the page by calling \`suggest_next_tasks\` with two or three changes you would recommend. Each change is a \`title\` of about four words, worded as the user would ask for it, and a quieter \`line\` that adds the detail the title leaves out: what changes, and where. Make each title name its own change, because the user reads them at a glance. Onlook draws the cards directly beneath your last reply, so that reply hands them over: what you changed or saw, where to look, and why these are worth doing next, for this page and not as a pitch. Do not sign off as if nothing is left, ask “Want me to…?”, or list the tasks in prose.
- Picture each task, in this order of preference. When it starts from something on a frame, an element it changes or one on a reference site it builds from, read that frame with \`read_frame\`, then name the frame and the ref of an element in view, and Onlook clips a real screenshot of it; a card about a frame always names the frame, so it shows the frame even when the element cannot be clipped. When it adds something with nothing on a frame to start from, a new screen, section or step, \`sketch\` it: block out where its parts would sit. If you are not confident a sketch reads as the idea, give no picture; the task shows as text, and that is fine.
- Say what changed in designer words — spacing, hierarchy, contrast, color — not file names. Files on request.
- One idea per sentence. Plain words. Active voice. No jargon the user did not use.

You have memory across sessions: what you write here is still there the next time you work on this repository — even in a different Onlook project made from it, a new worktree, a fresh session. Use it to remember what a designer already told you: the codebase, its stack, their taste, decisions already made — so you stop asking what memory already answers.

This store is not private to Onlook. It is the same memory the user's own \`claude\` reads and writes for this repository, so write only what you would want them to find in their own notes — durable facts in your own words, not a log of what happened.

Never write: secrets, API keys, tokens, or anything from a \`.env\` file; absolute paths on this machine — including this worktree, its branch, or its port, none of which carry over to another project; the user's name, email, or anything else that identifies them personally; or anything from this project that is not committed — another project made from the same repository reads this memory without ever seeing that work, so writing it as fact is writing something false there.

# Turn

Before your first tool call, tell me in one short line what you are about to do: look through the code to work out how to run it, then put it on the canvas. Someone new to Onlook cannot tell that reading files means you are setting up their app. First call get_project_setup. Reuse saved setup with run_project_setup. If none exists, detect runtime, package manager, install inputs/outputs and environment prerequisites, save_project_setup with repeatable preparation and foreground server commands, then run_project_setup. On failure, find the cause in the retained actual result. Running the same commands again needs no question. A repair that changes how the project installs or runs is a proposal: a different command or package, a flag such as --ignore-scripts, or an edit to its code or config. Tell me what failed and what you would change, your pick first, and ask me before you save or run it; once I agree, save with the current expectedRevision and run again. A user Stop cancels this request and is not a failure to repair. Do not launch a session-owned background server. Get this project running so I can design against it on the canvas, live in a browser frame. Onlook measures readiness and places the app when run_project_setup succeeds. Use server_running only for an already-existing server outside the saved setup runtime. If list_frames shows no frame yet, put one on the canvas early, before the setup runs: create_frame with pending set to true and a url on the port you expect. Then tell me in a line that my app will appear in that frame once it is running. When run_project_setup succeeds, Onlook points that frame at the app, so never create a second frame for it. Before you finish, make sure the app is in a frame.

Onlook looked at this repository before this turn. Packages with a \`dev\` script (name, path, framework, port):
- web at apps/web (next), no port set; the framework's default is 3000
- docs at apps/docs (vite), port 5174, set by the project
Before starting anything, ask with \`AskUserQuestion\`: which app to run — one option per package above, labelled with its name, with the path, framework and port as listed above in the description. Start nothing until the answer arrives."
`;

exports[`what a first-run Claude session is told, with every moment on in every session, as its Studio tools 1`] = `
"## get_project_setup

Read this project's saved setup, verified revision and retained failure. Takes no project path.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {},
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## save_project_setup

Save a reusable setup script outside the repository. First read get_project_setup and pass saved.revision as expectedRevision, or null for no saved script. Include repeatable POSIX prepare activation and prerequisite checks, installation input files and output sentinels, and foreground servers with contained relative cwd and fixed ports. Never embed credentials. Saving does not verify execution. Keep commands in the foreground without nohup or shell backgrounding.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "definition": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "prepare": {
          "type": "string",
          "maxLength": 65536
        },
        "install": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "script": {
              "type": "string",
              "maxLength": 65536
            },
            "inputs": {
              "type": "array",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              }
            },
            "outputs": {
              "type": "array",
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              }
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "script",
            "inputs",
            "outputs"
          ],
          "additionalProperties": false
        },
        "servers": {
          "minItems": 1,
          "maxItems": 32,
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
              "id": {
                "type": "string",
                "minLength": 1
              },
              "cwd": {
                "type": "string",
                "minLength": 1
              },
              "start": {
                "type": "string",
                "minLength": 1,
                "maxLength": 65536
              },
              "port": {
                "type": "integer",
                "minimum": 1,
                "maximum": 65535
              }
            },
            "required": [
              "id",
              "cwd",
              "start",
              "port"
            ],
            "additionalProperties": false
          }
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "prepare",
        "install",
        "servers"
      ],
      "additionalProperties": false
    },
    "expectedRevision": {
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "definition",
    "expectedRevision"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## run_project_setup

Start, stop or restart the saved setup using Onlook-owned processes independent of this conversation. Installation is reused only when input hashes and output sentinels match. Returns the actual retained command failure or measured readiness. Optional serverIds restrict the operation to saved server ids.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "action": {
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
        "start",
        "stop",
        "restart"
      ]
    },
    "serverIds": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "type": "string"
      }
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "action"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## server_running

Tell Onlook that this project's development server is now running, so the app can show it as started. Call this once the server has actually bound its port — not when you have merely launched the command. Always pass the port it is listening on when you know it; Onlook verifies the port itself and will show nothing as running until something answers there. If you started it somewhere other than the project root — an app directory in a monorepo — pass that directory as cwd, so Onlook can tell two servers in one project apart. If Onlook's own starter page is still running for this project, reporting a different port retires it. The reply says whether Onlook put the app on the canvas.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "port": {
      "description": "The TCP port the development server is listening on, e.g. 3000. Omit only if it genuinely cannot be determined.",
      "type": "integer",
      "minimum": 1,
      "maximum": 65535
    },
    "cwd": {
      "description": "The directory the server was started in, if it is not the project root — e.g. apps/web in a monorepo. Relative to the project, or absolute inside it.",
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1
    }
  },
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## frame_working

Tell Onlook which frame you are about to change and what you are changing on it. While you work, the user sees that frame marked as in progress on the canvas. Once your turn ends, Onlook lists what you finished, one line per frame read at a glance: "<done> <frame name>’s <what>". Call it right before each change to what a frame shows, not after, and again for each separate change on the same frame. Nothing else depends on the result.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "frame_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The frame you are about to change, exactly as list_frames reported its id."
    },
    "done": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 40,
      "description": "The verb for this change once it is made, in the past tense, capitalized, e.g. \\"Recolored\\"."
    },
    "what": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 60,
      "description": "The thing on the page this change acts on, in a few words with no article, named the way a designer looking at the page would name it. It reads mid-sentence, so lowercase unless it is a name, e.g. \\"signup button\\". Never a tag, selector, class, component or file name."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "frame_id",
    "done",
    "what"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## waiting_on

Tell Onlook what you need from the user, right before you ask them a question, so the status the user sees says what you are waiting on while your question waits for them. Nothing else depends on the result.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "what": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 80,
      "description": "What you need from the user, in a few words a designer reads at a glance, lowercase, e.g. \\"a color for the signup button\\". Onlook shows it as \\"Waiting on <what>\\". Never a tag, selector, class, component or file name."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "what"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## set_frame_url

Point one frame on the Onlook canvas at a URL, so the user sees this page in it. Call this whenever the development server is running somewhere other than where a frame is currently pointed — most often because it bound a different port than the default — or when the user asks to see a particular page in a frame they already have. Call list_frames first if you do not know the frame id; to add a frame rather than move one, use create_frame.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "frame_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The frame to point somewhere else, exactly as list_frames reported its id."
    },
    "url": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The address to show, e.g. http://localhost:5173. A bare port (5173) or a host:port (localhost:5173) is also accepted."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "frame_id",
    "url"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## rename_project

Name this project in Onlook's project list and tab, and move its git branch to match. Call it once, when a project has no name yet and you are told to name it; a project the user has named, or that was named already, keeps its name and the call is skipped. The name is at most three words.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "name": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The name, at most three words, derived from what the user asked for — e.g. \\"Pricing Page\\" or \\"Portfolio Redesign\\". Longer names are cut to three words."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "name"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## create_project_tab

Open a second project in a new Onlook tab: another isolated copy of this repository, in the same workspace as this project, so work can go on in parallel without touching what is on screen. Takes no arguments: the repository is this project's own. The tab opens in the background and the conversation stays where it is. The result carries a link to the new tab — refer to it by that link so the user can jump to it. To name the new project, call rename_project from its own session. If the repository cannot be isolated the result says why; there is no other way to open one.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {},
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## suggest_next_tasks

Offer the user the changes you recommend for this page, as cards they can choose. Call this as the last thing in every turn that changed the page. Give two or three tasks, each a concrete next change that follows from what you saw while working: a short title worded as their own request, and a line with the detail the title leaves out. A chosen task arrives as their next message, its title as the text and its line beside it. You do not see the screen: Onlook draws the cards directly beneath your last reply, so that reply hands them over. Write it as someone offering these options, not as someone who has finished with nothing left to do. A picture lets the user see a task’s idea at a glance before reading it. A task that starts from something on a frame, a part of the page it changes or a part of a reference site it builds from, names that frame and the ref read_frame gave that part, and Onlook clips it. A task with nothing on a frame to start from, such as a new screen, section or step, carries a sketch instead: a clip could only show what exists.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "tasks": {
      "minItems": 1,
      "maxItems": 3,
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "title": {
            "type": "string",
            "minLength": 1,
            "maxLength": 60,
            "description": "The task in about four words, as the user would ask for it, e.g. \\"Tighten the hero spacing\\"."
          },
          "line": {
            "type": "string",
            "minLength": 1,
            "maxLength": 100,
            "description": "One short line under the title with the detail it leaves out: what changes, and where."
          },
          "picture": {
            "description": "What the card pictures beside this task.",
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
              "frame_id": {
                "description": "The frame, from list_frames, showing the page this task changes or starts from, local or any other site. Onlook clips its picture for the card from it, and shows the whole frame when it cannot clip the element.",
                "type": "string",
                "minLength": 1
              },
              "ref": {
                "description": "The element this task changes or starts from, by its ref from read_frame on this frame, such as e12; pick one in view. Used only to picture it; the title and line stay in the user's words, never the ref or an element name.",
                "type": "string",
                "pattern": "^e[1-9][0-9]*$"
              },
              "selector": {
                "description": "A CSS selector for the element, only when read_frame gave it no ref; give a ref or a selector, not both. Leave both out to picture the whole frame.",
                "type": "string",
                "minLength": 1,
                "maxLength": 500
              },
              "sketch": {
                "description": "A rough blocking of what this task adds, for a task that adds something not on the page yet, only when you are confident it reads as the idea; otherwise give no picture and the task shows as text: up to 12 shapes on a box 100 across and 100 down, drawn on a tile a little wider than it is tall. Block out where the parts of the idea sit, not their details: a new phone screen is an outline holding a heading, two text lines and a block for its button. Onlook draws the shapes in its own style, with no color or words.",
                "minItems": 1,
                "maxItems": 12,
                "type": "array",
                "items": {
                  "type": "object",
                  "properties": {
                    "kind": {
                      "type": "string",
                      "enum": [
                        "heading",
                        "text",
                        "block",
                        "image",
                        "circle",
                        "outline"
                      ],
                      "description": "heading or text: a line of words, as long as \`w\`. block: a filled area such as a button, a bar or a card. image: a photo or an illustration. circle: an avatar, a dot or an icon, \`w\` across. outline: an empty box such as a phone screen, a panel or an input."
                    },
                    "x": {
                      "type": "number",
                      "minimum": 0,
                      "maximum": 100,
                      "description": "The left edge, from 0 to 100."
                    },
                    "y": {
                      "type": "number",
                      "minimum": 0,
                      "maximum": 100,
                      "description": "The top edge, from 0 to 100."
                    },
                    "w": {
                      "type": "number",
                      "exclusiveMinimum": 0,
                      "maximum": 100,
                      "description": "The width, from 0 to 100."
                    },
                    "h": {
                      "type": "number",
                      "exclusiveMinimum": 0,
                      "maximum": 100,
                      "description": "The height, for block, image and outline, from 0 to 100."
                    }
                  },
                  "required": [
                    "kind",
                    "x",
                    "y",
                    "w"
                  ],
                  "additionalProperties": false
                }
              }
            },
            "additionalProperties": false
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "title",
          "line"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false
      },
      "description": "Two or three tasks, at most 3, the one you would do first leading."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "tasks"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## list_frames

List every object on the Onlook canvas: its \`kind\` — "browser" for a frame, "text" for words written on the canvas, "image" for a picture beside them — with id, name, position and size, the canvas page it is on, and its stack rank within that canvas page (0 is the back-most object on it; higher paints on top). A frame also reports the address it is homed at, where it has actually navigated to, whether it loaded, its title, any console output since you last looked, and a \`pageError\` whenever it is showing something other than the site. A \`pageError\` carries the dev server's error screen — its message, and the file when the screen names one — or says the frame rendered nothing at all, or that it could not be read. It is read from the frame’s page itself each time this tool is called, not accumulated, so it disappears on its own once the frame renders again. A frame that loaded reports no \`pageError\` at all when the site is fine. A text object reports its words, a picture its description. Every canvas page is reported, including the ones the user is not looking at; pass page_id to narrow it to one. A canvas page holding nothing appears here not at all — list_pages is what reports the pages themselves. Call this before any other canvas tool, and again after changing the code — it is how you SEE what the user sees, rather than assuming an edit worked.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "page_id": {
      "description": "Report only this page’s objects. Omit to report every page.",
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1
    }
  },
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## list_pages

List this project’s canvas pages, in the order the user sees them: each page’s id, name, how many objects it holds, whether it is a divider (a rule between groups, not a place objects live), and which one the user is currently looking at. You can read and write objects on every page, whichever one that is. Call this before move_nodes_to_page or delete_page, and whenever you need a page a list_frames reading cannot show you — an empty one, or one added since this conversation started.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {},
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## create_page

Add a page to this project’s canvas, so a separate piece of work gets its own named place rather than crowding what is already on screen. The new page goes last and the user stays where they are — Onlook never moves their view for you, so name the page you used when you report back. The result carries its id; pass that id to the create verbs to put objects on it.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "name": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "What the page is for, in the user’s words — e.g. \\"Checkout\\". Two pages may share a name; the id is what identifies one. The exact text \\"---\\" makes a divider instead of a page: a rule the user sees between groups, which holds no objects."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "name"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## rename_page

Rename a canvas page. The name is what the user reads in the page list, so name a page after the work it holds. Renaming it to "---" makes it a divider — the same grouping the user has.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "page_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The page id, exactly as list_pages reported it."
    },
    "name": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "What the page should read instead, e.g. \\"Checkout — mobile\\". The exact text \\"---\\" turns it into a divider; a project keeps at least one page that is not one."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "page_id",
    "name"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## reorder_page

Change where a page sits in the Onlook page list. It moves nothing on the canvas and the user can undo it. A page you just added goes last; move it where it belongs rather than leaving that to the user.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "page_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The page id, exactly as list_pages reported it."
    },
    "position": {
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
        "first",
        "last",
        "earlier",
        "later"
      ],
      "description": "first: above every page. last: below every page. earlier/later: past the one page directly above or below it."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "page_id",
    "position"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## move_nodes_to_page

Move canvas objects — frames, text, pictures — onto another page, as one action the user undoes in one step. Each object keeps its position and size; only the page it belongs to changes. One id that names no object moves nothing. A divider holds no objects and is refused.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "object_ids": {
      "minItems": 1,
      "maxItems": 1000,
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "type": "string",
        "minLength": 1
      },
      "description": "One to 1000 unique object ids, exactly as list_frames reported them."
    },
    "page_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The page id, exactly as list_pages reported it."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "object_ids",
    "page_id"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## delete_page

Delete a canvas page and every object on it. This closes those views — it never deletes any of the user’s code or files, and the user can undo it in one step. To keep the objects, move them with move_nodes_to_page first. A project keeps at least one page.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "page_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The page id, exactly as list_pages reported it."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "page_id"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## create_frame

Add a frame to the Onlook canvas, so the user can see another page beside the ones already there. Give a url, or copy_of to duplicate an existing frame. Prefer beside to place it next to a specific frame; give x/y only when the arrangement itself is the point. The result reports whether the new frame overlaps another on the same page and, when Onlook can tell, whether it is currently visible — refer to it by its link afterwards so the user can jump to it either way. Once your turn ends, Onlook lists each frame you added as one line of finished work, "<done> <frame name>", so give the verb here rather than marking the frame you copied from.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "url": {
      "description": "The address the new frame shows, e.g. http://localhost:5173/pricing. A bare port (5173) or host:port is also accepted. Required unless copy_of is given; passed alongside copy_of it overrides the copied address.",
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1
    },
    "copy_of": {
      "description": "The id of a frame to copy. The copy inherits its size and address, and the canvas records where it came from.",
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1
    },
    "name": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The page or view the frame shows, in a word or two, as a designer would name it, e.g. \\"Checkout\\", or \\"Checkout mobile\\" for a variant. No site name, address or id."
    },
    "x": {
      "description": "Canvas x. Not combined with beside. Omit x, y and beside to let Onlook place the frame — beside its source when copying, otherwise to the right of the rightmost frame.",
      "type": "number"
    },
    "y": {
      "description": "Canvas y. Only used when x is given too.",
      "type": "number"
    },
    "beside": {
      "description": "The id of a frame to land next to, exactly as list_frames reported it. Not combined with x/y.",
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1
    },
    "side": {
      "description": "Which side of \`beside\` to land on. Defaults to right.",
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
        "right",
        "below",
        "left",
        "above"
      ]
    },
    "page_id": {
      "description": "The page to put it on, exactly as list_pages reported its id. Omit it and the object lands on the page the user is currently looking at, or — with beside or copy_of — on the page that object lives on. An id naming no page, a divider, or a page other than the anchor’s is refused rather than placed somewhere else.",
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1
    },
    "done": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 40,
      "description": "The verb for adding the frame, in the past tense, capitalized, e.g. \\"Added\\" or \\"Sketched\\"."
    },
    "quote": {
      "description": "What you are making for this page, in one sentence, with every choice settled, including the ones the user left to you. Name the actual thing as if it already had its title and its one telling detail: who it is for, what it covers, what it is used for. Never repeat their answers back. When they said to make it up, or that you choose, the quote says what you chose, and the user should feel the vague idea they gave you turned into something real. At most 160 characters. The new frame shows it while you build the page, so the user sees their idea taken up before anything is built, and Onlook takes it down the first time \`capture_frame\` shows you what is under it, or when your turn ends, unless that turn ends on a question for the user. Give it when you start building a page whose brief is settled, not before your questions are answered.",
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 160
    },
    "pending": {
      "description": "True when the app is not running yet, as while you set the project up. The frame waits under its quote, and Onlook points it at the app on whatever port the setup reports, keeping the url’s path. With pending, the quote names the app that will appear here.",
      "type": "boolean"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "name",
    "done"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## clear_frame_quote

Take down the quote a frame or an artifact shows, when what it describes will not be built there after all. Onlook takes a quote down by itself the first time \`capture_frame\` shows you what is under it, or when your turn ends, unless that turn ends on a question for the user. frame_id is the canvas id of either, as list_frames reports it: for an artifact, the \`id\` of its placement, not its \`artifactId\`.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "frame_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The frame id, exactly as list_frames reported it."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "frame_id"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## move_frame

Move or resize a frame on the Onlook canvas. Use it to arrange frames the user asked to compare, or to give one a phone-sized viewport. Prefer beside to land next to a specific frame; give x/y only when the arrangement itself is the point — x grows to the right and y grows downward. The result reports whether the frame now overlaps another on the same page. With quote, it also puts the brief of a page you are about to build over the frame.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "frame_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The frame id, exactly as list_frames reported it."
    },
    "x": {
      "description": "New canvas x. Not combined with beside. Omit to keep the current one.",
      "type": "number"
    },
    "y": {
      "description": "New canvas y. Not combined with beside. Omit to keep the current one.",
      "type": "number"
    },
    "width": {
      "description": "New viewport width in px — the site’s area, excluding the frame chrome. Passing a size resizes and moves in one step.",
      "type": "number",
      "exclusiveMinimum": 0
    },
    "height": {
      "description": "New viewport height in px.",
      "type": "number",
      "exclusiveMinimum": 0
    },
    "beside": {
      "description": "The id of a frame to land next to, exactly as list_frames reported it. Not combined with x/y.",
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1
    },
    "side": {
      "description": "Which side of \`beside\` to land on. Defaults to right.",
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
        "right",
        "below",
        "left",
        "above"
      ]
    },
    "quote": {
      "description": "What you are making for this page, in one sentence, with every choice settled, including the ones the user left to you. Name the actual thing as if it already had its title and its one telling detail: who it is for, what it covers, what it is used for. Never repeat their answers back. When they said to make it up, or that you choose, the quote says what you chose, and the user should feel the vague idea they gave you turned into something real. At most 160 characters. Give it when you are about to build a page into this frame or artifact: the user then sees the brief over it, not the page half-built, and Onlook takes it down the first time \`capture_frame\` shows you what is under it, or when your turn ends, unless that turn ends on a question for the user. Give the size the page needs in the same call. Pointing the frame at another address with set_frame_url takes the quote down, so do that first.",
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 160
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "frame_id"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

