## duplicate_frames

Duplicate several canvas frames as one atomic action, preserving their relative geometry. Omit offset_x and offset_y to let Onlook place each copy beside its own source; give both only when the arrangement itself is the point. Once your turn ends, Onlook lists each frame you added as one line of finished work, "<done> <frame name>", so give the verb here rather than marking the frame you copied from. Rename a copy once you know what it will show; its line reads the name.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "frame_ids": {
      "minItems": 1,
      "maxItems": 1000,
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "type": "string",
        "minLength": 1,
        "description": "The frame id, exactly as list_frames reported it."
      },
      "description": "One to 1000 unique frame ids, in operation order."
    },
    "offset_x": {
      "description": "Canvas x offset applied to every copy. Omit both offsets to let Onlook place each copy beside its source.",
      "type": "number"
    },
    "offset_y": {
      "description": "Canvas y offset applied to every copy. Omit both offsets to let Onlook place each copy beside its source.",
      "type": "number"
    },
    "done": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 40,
      "description": "The verb for adding the frame, in the past tense, capitalized, e.g. \\"Added\\" or \\"Sketched\\"."
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "frame_ids",
    "done"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## rename_frame

Rename a frame on the Onlook canvas. The name is what the user sees in the frame list, so name frames after what they SHOW rather than after their address. Onlook also reads it inside short status lines, so keep it short.

{
