## evaluate_in_frame

Run JavaScript inside a frame’s page and get the result back, so you can CHECK what actually rendered instead of assuming an edit worked — computed styles, element text, how many rows a list has. Runs in any web page a frame shows, your dev server or another site, and in Artifacts on the Canvas. list_frames shows you where each frame currently is. To put the page in a state and then see it — a moment mid-animation, an open menu, a scrolled position — use capture_frame with setup_script instead, so it is one call. A reload goes in its own call, returning straight away — (() => { location.reload(); return 'reloading'; })() — with the read in the next call.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "frame_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The frame id, exactly as list_frames reported it."
    },
    "script": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "JavaScript to evaluate in the page. The value of the last expression is returned as JSON, so end with the thing you want to see — e.g. getComputedStyle(document.querySelector('h1')).color. Scripts share one scope per frame across calls, so a top-level const/let collides with an earlier call — wrap your script in an IIFE: (() => { const el = …; return el.textContent; })()"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "frame_id",
    "script"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## read_frame

List what a frame’s page shows as one line per element: its role, its name, and a ref such as [ref=e12]. Lists links, buttons, form fields, headings, named images, cards and the landmarks that hold them, on any page a frame shows. A ref names that element for as long as the page stays loaded, so pass it to suggest_next_tasks to picture the element on a card. An element marked [below], [above], [offscreen] or [partly] is not in view, and a card cannot picture it.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "frame_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The frame id, exactly as list_frames reported it."
    },
    "scope": {
      "description": "view, the default, lists what is in view and counts the rest; page lists the whole page, marking what is out of view.",
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
        "view",
        "page"
      ]
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "frame_id"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

## capture_frame

Take a screenshot of what a frame is currently rendering, so you can SEE the result of a change rather than inferring it from the code. Use it for layout, spacing, overflow and visual regressions — things the DOM will not tell you. Captures whatever page the frame shows: your dev server, a LAN or tailnet address, or an external site. When the page needs to be in a state first, pass setup_script rather than calling evaluate_in_frame before this: the user sees one step instead of two.

{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "frame_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "description": "The frame id, exactly as list_frames reported it."
    },
    "setup_script": {
      "description": "Optional JavaScript Onlook runs in the page first, then captures, so setting up a state and seeing it is one call: pause an animation at a moment, open a menu, scroll to a section. Return a promise to have the capture wait for it. Runs wherever evaluate_in_frame runs. Scripts share one scope per frame across calls, so a top-level const/let collides with an earlier call — wrap your script in an IIFE: (() => { const el = …; return el.textContent; })()",
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "frame_id"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}

